WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.520
How does zero-one knapsack dynamic

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.400
programming enforce single use? We

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.740
will derive an include-or-exclude recurrence,

4
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.260
build a complete small table,

5
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.200
recover the chosen items, and

6
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.540
identify the update that would

7
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.220
accidentally permit reuse.

8
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.060
Each item is indivisible and

9
00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:18.500
may be selected at most

10
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.920
once. Capacity is an upper

11
00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:23.040
limit, so an optimal solution

12
00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.180
may leave room unused; filling

13
00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:26.880
the bag exactly is not

14
00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:27.420
required.

15
00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.380
Let P at c and

16
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:30.980
j be the maximum profit

17
00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.320
attainable with capacity c using

18
00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:34.680
only the first j items.

19
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:36.700
With no items or with

20
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.880
zero capacity, the value is

21
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.980
zero under the positive-weight assumptions.

22
00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.140
If item j is too

23
00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.860
heavy, it cannot be included,

24
00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.700
so copy the value using

25
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.200
the first j minus one

26
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:51.000
items. Otherwise compare excluding it

27
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.600
with its profit plus the

28
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:53.960
best value at the remaining

29
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.300
capacity, again using only the

30
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:57.980
first j minus one items.

31
00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.700
Both branches decrease the item

32
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.500
index, which is the reason

33
00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:04.880
single use is enforced. Once

34
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:06.320
item j has been included,

35
00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.900
its subproblem cannot include that

36
00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:10.040
same item again.

37
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.200
Our capacity is five, and

38
00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.780
items have weight-profit pairs two

39
00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.420
and three, three and four,

40
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:19.200
and four and five. Begin

41
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:20.940
with the empty column, then

42
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.620
add one available item at

43
00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.280
a time while preserving the

44
00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:26.160
already computed previous column.

45
00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:28.640
With only item one available,

46
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:31.300
capacities zero and one cannot

47
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.700
fit it, so they stay

48
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.540
zero. At capacities two through

49
00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:35.980
five the profit is three;

50
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.060
the bag never gets two

51
00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.460
copies just because a larger

52
00:01:39.460 --> 00:01:40.600
capacity could hold them.

53
00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:42.900
Add item two, whose weight

54
00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:44.340
is three and profit is

55
00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.320
four. At capacity three it

56
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.840
improves the profit to four,

57
00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.420
at capacity four it still

58
00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:51.200
gives four, and at capacity

59
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.660
five combining it with item

60
00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:54.040
one yields seven.

61
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:56.820
Add item three, whose weight

62
00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:58.320
is four and profit is

63
00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.680
five. It improves capacity four

64
00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:02.200
from four to five, but

65
00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.040
at capacity five its inclusion

66
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.020
gives only five, so excluding

67
00:02:06.020 --> 00:02:07.360
it preserves the better value

68
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:07.720
seven.

69
00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.140
Reconstruct from capacity five and

70
00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.160
all three items. The value

71
00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.280
equals the previous column, so

72
00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.080
skip item three; with the

73
00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.500
first two items the value

74
00:02:18.500 --> 00:02:20.360
seven exceeds the first-item value

75
00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:22.420
three, so select item two

76
00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.340
and reduce capacity by three.

77
00:02:24.820 --> 00:02:26.760
At capacity two and item

78
00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:29.020
one, the value three exceeds

79
00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:31.660
the no-item value zero, so

80
00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.220
select item one. The recovered

81
00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.220
pair has total weight five

82
00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.940
and profit seven; decrease the

83
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.840
item index after either taking

84
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:41.620
or skipping.

85
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:43.550
The table has order n

86
00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.820
C states, each comparing at

87
00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:48.800
most two constant-time alternatives, giving

88
00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.020
big O of n C

89
00:02:50.020 --> 00:02:52.780
time and space. Reconstruction takes

90
00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.500
at most n item decisions,

91
00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:56.520
while a memoised version must

92
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.400
also account for recursive storage.

93
00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:02.100
A one-dimensional optimisation processes capacities

94
00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.320
downward for each item, so

95
00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:05.960
a transition reads a value

96
00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.100
from before that item was

97
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.420
added. Updating upward can read

98
00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.560
a newly improved smaller-capacity cell

99
00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:13.580
and reuse the item, silently

100
00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:15.440
changing the problem to unbounded

101
00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:16.020
knapsack.

102
00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:17.480
Things to take note of:

103
00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.380
define both state dimensions, guard

104
00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:21.960
the overweight branch, and keep

105
00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:23.380
j minus one in both

106
00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:27.060
choices. Distinguish zero-one, fractional and

107
00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:30.260
unlimited-item variants, allow unused capacity,

108
00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:32.880
and explain the numeric-capacity cost

109
00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.240
before calling this algorithm polynomial.
